// Failure golden: a `Path` endpoint written as an APPLICATION is the endpoint
// the checker sees, and a body that does not inhabit that identity type is
// REFUSED.
//
// The witness matters because of what it replaces. An application endpoint was
// previously out of the type-position fragment, and an out-of-fragment type
// degraded to the gradual unknown -- which is consistent with everything, so
// this program type-checked CLEAN against a type its own source refutes. There
// was no diagnostic, no goal, and nothing to notice. The type here states that
// `dbl(x)` and `x` are identified; `ret 5` inhabits neither that nor anything
// like it, and the diagnostic now names the endpoints it was checked against.
//
// The two neighbouring pins are `undefined-endpoint-head.gandr`, where the
// endpoint names a function that does not exist, and this file's positive
// counterpart in the model tree, where variable endpoints check as they always
// did.
//
// (Pathological tree: testing artifact, not pedagogy -- ADR-52 Decision C.)

def dbl(x: Integer) -> F Integer {
  ret (x + x)
}

def refuted(x: Integer) -> F Path(Integer, dbl(x), x) {
  ret 5
}

//@ expect-diagnostic: type mismatch
